: "https://media.scored.co/post/cBEKq2GmdZnZ.gif", "author_flair_class": "", "is_removed": false, "media": {"resized_resolutions": [{"reference": "/scale/X1zD7SnptQC9JBgF.mp4", "width": 781, "content-type": "mp4", "height": 779}], "blurhash": "U8F=gmIn4oE2}baex[fhieWX-:tR?wS1E2o~", "full_resolution": [779, 781]}, "title": "They come from failed countries ", "last_comment_created": 1795785805244, "content": "", "score_down": 0, "author_flair_text": "", "pro_tier": 0, "is_stickied": false, "is_nsfw": false, "is_deleted": false, "is_image": true, "comments": 1, "score_up": 13, "is_edited": false, "is_moderator": false, "awards": 0, "vote_state": 2, "link_metadata": {"status": 200}, "post_flair_text": "", "archive_id": ""}, {"is_locked": false, "tweet_id": "", "sticky_comment": 0, "is_video_mp4": false, "type": "text", "uuid": "1ATon64x4q", "is_twitter": false, "is_admin": false, "score": 14, "suggested_sort": 0, "post_flair_class": "", "id": 8957178, "raw_content": "When Europeans first arrived, North American Native American weaponry was relying on materials like stone, bone, flint, chert, obsidian, and wood. Before the widespread introduction of European goods, tribes utilized a diverse arsenal including the bow and arrow, spears, atlatls (spear-throwers), war clubs, tomahawks (stone-headed axes), and slingshots.\n\nThere were copper weapons like knives and arrowheads, but many of these were largely ceremonial and highly localized.\n\nThe oldest known example is an 8,500-year-old copper projectile point from Eagle Lake, Wisconsin. \u2060 Other artifacts include copper spear points, arrowheads, knives, axes, adzes, harpoons, and awls, all cold-hammered from native copper nuggets. \n\n\u2060By the time of Spanish contact, the Aztecs had developed bronze-smelting. \u2060 During the 1520 civil conflict, Cort\u00e9s had indigenous metalworkers cast bronze-tipped spears.\n\nTrue bronze smelting \u2014 alloying copper with tin or arsenic \u2014 is documented only in Mesoamerica and the Andes, and even there it was used more for tools, ornaments, and prestige objects than for mass-producing weapons.\n", "author": "Moebius", "created": 1795780668550, "profile_picture": "0F9hm63l5dUw", "community": "TheDonald", "ad_tier": 2, "last_comment_author": "", "is_video": false, "video_link": "", "is_new_user": false, "domain": "", "is_crosspost": false, "preview": "", "removal_source": "", "link": "", "author_flair_class": "", "is_removed": false, "media": {"resized_resolutions": [], "blurhash": "", "full_resolution": []}, "title": "When Europeans Made First Contact with Native Americans They Were Barely Using Copper for Weapons...Still Predominately Using Stone Age Weapons.", "last_comment_created": 1795780668550, "content": "<p>When Europeans first arrived, North American Native American weaponry was relying on materials like stone, bone, flint, chert, obsidian, and wood. Before the widespread introduction of European goods, tribes utilized a diverse arsenal including the bow and arrow, spears, atlatls (spear-throwers), war clubs, tomahawks (stone-headed axes), and slingshots.</p>\n<p>There were copper weapons like knives and arrowheads, but many of these were largely ceremonial and highly localized.</p>\n<p>The oldest known example is an 8,500-year-old copper projectile point from Eagle Lake, Wisconsin. \u2060 Other artifacts include copper spear points, arrowheads, knives, axes, adzes, harpoons, and awls, all cold-hammered from native copper nuggets.</p>\n<p>\u2060By the time of Spanish contact, the Aztecs had developed bronze-smelting. \u2060 During the 1520 civil conflict, Cort\u00e9s had indigenous metalworkers cast bronze-tipped spears.</p>\n<p>True bronze smelting \u2014 alloying copper with tin or arsenic \u2014 is documented only in Mesoamerica and the Andes, and even there it was used more for tools, ornaments, and prestige objects than for mass-producing weapons.</p>\n", "score_down": 0, "author_flair_text": "", "pro_tier": 0, "is_s